---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import InterestForm from "../components/InterestForm.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Buying property in Andorra as a non-resident | Official notes"
  description="Non-resident purchases in Andorra: AGIA-licensed agents, notaries, and foreign-investment authorisation as published by the Government. No invented prices or tax rates."
  path="/buying-property"
>
  <section class="page-header">
    <h1>Buying property as a non-resident</h1>
    <p>
      A house is not a residence permit, and a permit is not a right to buy.
      Non-resident purchases sit under foreign-investment rules that the
      Government has tightened in recent laws.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        We omit current tax percentages, unit caps and investment floors. Those
        have been revised in successive laws and regulations. Confirm the live
        test with the Government, a notary and an
        <a href="https://www.agia.ad/collegiats" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
          >AGIA</a
        >-registered agent.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Who typically needs authorisation</h2>
    <p>
      Official Government publications describe prior administrative
      authorisation for foreign investment in real estate. In the Government’s
      own statistical yearbook the cases include a non-resident individual who
      is not Andorran, and a resident who cannot show three years of effective
      and permanent residence within the ten years before the application —
      subject to international conventions. Treat that as a map, not the
      current legal test. The operative text is the foreign-investment law and
      its regulations, as applied by the Register of Foreign Investments.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Government has published a
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/w/govern-aprova-un-nou-reglament-inversio-estrangera"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">regulation on foreign investment</a
      >
      giving effect to Law 5/2025 on sustainable growth and the right to
      housing, and a later
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/w/s-actualitza-el-reglament-de-l-impost-sobre-la-inversio-estrangera-immobiliaria-d-acord-amb-la-nova-llei-de-continuitat-i-consolidacio-de-les-mesures-per-al-creixement-sostenible"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">update to the foreign-investment property tax</a
      >. Those pages describe tighter control of foreign purchases and a tax
      on foreign real-estate investment. We do not reprint the percentages:
      read the current regulation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Applications for related company and investment filings are described as
      going through the Government’s service counters or
      <a href="https://www.e-tramits.ad/tramits/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        >e-tramits.ad</a
      >. A notary formalises a completed purchase.
    </p>

    <h2>Use a licensed agent</h2>
    <p>
      Estate agents must be authorised and listed by
      <a href="https://www.agia.ad/collegiats" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        >AGIA</a
      >. We list only member firms that publish their own sites, in the
      <a href="/directory">directory</a>.
      This site does not invent houses or prices; see
      <a href="/properties">how we will list homes</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Housing for a residence file</h2>
    <p>
      Immigration pages ask for proof of accommodation — a lease, a title, or
      another accepted certificate. Buying can satisfy that, once the
      investment and the notarial deed are in order. The permit itself is
      still
      <a href="/become-a-resident">an immigration application</a>.
      Parish context:
      <a href="/parishes">the seven parishes</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <InterestForm
    intro="Leave your details if you are looking at a home in Andorra and will need a licensed agent or counsel."
    source="buying-property"
  />
</Base>
